"S.Bridle.1","A.Gude.1","A.Nicolis.1","C.Lidman.1","K.Barbary.1","Licia.Verde.1","L.Lubin.1","Anne.Christine.Davis.1","Eiichiro.Komatsu.1","P.Brax.1","A.Diercks.2","C.R.Mullis.2","J.E.Gunn.1","S.A.Snedden.1","S.Pascoli.1","P.Ripoche.4","N.Yasuda.1","Charles.L.Bennett.1","A.Ali.6","S.Dodelson.1","Mark.W.Strovink.1","C.R.Pennypacker.1","L.Faccioli.4","Lyman.A.Page.1","H.K.Fakhouri.1","Nelson.J.Nunes.1","K.Tokita.4","Jason.Spyromilio.1","P.Eisenhardt.7","N.A.Roe.1","M.Postman.1","R.Amanullah.1","W.J.Couch.1","Ariel.Goobar.1","M.P.Kamionkowski.1","E.Malanushenko.2","J.Meyers.2","Florian.Beutler.1","M.B.Pracy.1","P.Castro.2","E.L.Wright.1","Thomas.G.Zlosnik.1","David.F.Mota.1","Rachel.Bean.1","Savvas.Nesseris.1","R.S.Ellis.1","Adam.G.Riess.1","S.J.Bailey.1","Bao.Jiu.Li.1","K.S.Dawson.1","Nobunari.Kashikawa.1","Reinabelle.Reyes.1","P.RuizLapuente.11","I.Sawicki.1","Daniel.Stern.1","N.Odegard.3","E.Ellingson.1","T.Pritchard.2","Edward.J.Wollack.1","David.J.Schlegel.1","V.Stanishev.1","T.K.Wyder.1","M.E.C.Swanson.1","C.Burrage.1","Reynald.Pain.1","A.L.Spadafora.1","A.De.Felice.1","R.S.Hill.2","N.P.Ross.2","M.Limon.2","Gregory.Aldering.1","H.J.Newberg.1","A.J.Cuesta.1","Carlton.M.Baugh.1","Tamara.M.Davis.1","L.L.Wang.4","M.Sandstad.1","C.K.McBride.1","Henk.Hoekstra.1","H.Brewington.1","R.A.Knop.2","Nicholas.B.Suntzeff.1","I.Zehavi.1","Robert.R.Caldwell.1","D.Rubin.1","Bradley.E.Schaefer.1","D.G.Gilbank.1","K.Honscheid.1","S.M.Croom.1","R.G.McMahon.1","Carlos.Contreras.1","R.Rattazzi.1","Benjamin.A.Weaver.1","Joanna.Dunkley.1","Uros.Seljak.1","R.Kase.1","S.A.Appleby.1"],"figures":[{"filename":"G_eff.png","caption":"Gravitational coupling deviation $\\geff/G_N$ and effective equation of state $w$ are shown for examples with high redshift initial conditions $\\rho_{\\pi,i}=10^{-6} \\rho_{\\rm m,i}$ (dashed) and $\\rho_{\\pi,i}=3\\times10^{-5} \\rho_{\\rm m,i}$ (solid). Note that adjusting $\\rho_{\\pi,i}$ to lessen deviations in gravity increases the deviations in equation of state, and vice versa.","source":"arxiv","key":"5ffbe909ce2ac9f713ab5f61f717d8bf","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/5ffbe909ce2ac9f713ab5f61f717d8bf"},{"filename":"w_eff.png","caption":"Gravitational coupling deviation $\\geff/G_N$ and effective equation of state $w$ are shown for examples with high redshift initial conditions $\\rho_{\\pi,i}=10^{-6} \\rho_{\\rm m,i}$ (dashed) and $\\rho_{\\pi,i}=3\\times10^{-5} \\rho_{\\rm m,i}$ (solid). Note that adjusting $\\rho_{\\pi,i}$ to lessen deviations in gravity increases the deviations in equation of state, and vice versa.","source":"arxiv","key":"41025061fd43c7d80f8e99d8a301992e","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/41025061fd43c7d80f8e99d8a301992e"},{"filename":"chi2plot.png","caption":"$\\Delta\\chi^2$ relative to the best fit \\lcdm\\ model (purple square) is shown for the Galileon model as a function of the CMB shift parameter $R$ (an example of expansion) and growth rate $f\\sigma_8(z=0.57)$ (an example of growth, measured by BOSS \\cite{reidboss}). Blue stars are points derived from the MCMC chains, outlining the rough paraboloid of the $\\chi^2$ surface, with the yellow triangle the best fit. No values of the Galileon parameters provide a fit with $\\Delta\\chi^2&amp;lt;30.7$ (horizontal grid).","source":"arxiv","key":"3c6bef12b9509c9e2a43d2aa7f3912fa","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/3c6bef12b9509c9e2a43d2aa7f3912fa"},{"filename":"G_bf.png","caption":"Gravitational strength $\\geff(z)$ and effective dark energy equation of state $w(z)$ are plotted for the Galileon model that best fits the current data. Different $\\geff$ superscripts correspond to the different modified Poisson equations in \\cite{applin}.","source":"arxiv","key":"34efb5cc0f8a2a8344127f42cb7da688","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/34efb5cc0f8a2a8344127f42cb7da688"},{"filename":"w_bf.png","caption":"Gravitational strength $\\geff(z)$ and effective dark energy equation of state $w(z)$ are plotted for the Galileon model that best fits the current data. Different $\\geff$ superscripts correspond to the different modified Poisson equations in \\cite{applin}.","source":"arxiv","key":"6bbafd74ac7094d79c9cdd9f5ac8330e","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/6bbafd74ac7094d79c9cdd9f5ac8330e"}],"legacy_version":"20200410130002.0","inspire_categories":[{"term":"Astrophysics"}],"first_author":{"full_name":"Appleby, Stephen A.","last_name":"Appleby","first_name":"Stephen A.","recid":1048718},"control_number":1111617,"dois":[{"value":"10.1088/1475-7516/2012/08/026"}],"document_type":["article"],"texkeys":["Appleby:2012ba"],"abstracts":[{"source":"arXiv","value":"Galileon gravity is a robust theoretical alternative to general relativity with a cosmological constant for explaining cosmic acceleration, with interesting properties such as having second order field equations and a shift symmetry. While either its predictions for the cosmic expansion or growth histories can approach standard \\Lambda CDM, we demonstrate the incompatibility of both doing so simultaneously. Already current observational constraints can severely disfavor an entire class of Galileon gravity models that do not couple directly to matter, ruling them out as an alternative to \\Lambda CDM.","abstract_source_suggest":{"input":"arXiv"}}],"primary_arxiv_category":["astro-ph.CO"],"titles":[{"title":"Trial of Galileon gravity by cosmological expansion and growth observations"},{"title":"Galileons on Trial"},{"source":"arXiv","title":"Trial of Galileon gravity by cosmological expansion and growth observations"}],"curated":false}},{"links":{"bibtex":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/2828346?format=bibtex","latex-eu":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/2828346?format=latex-eu","latex-us":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/2828346?format=latex-us","json":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/2828346?format=json","json-expanded":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/2828346?format=json-expanded","cv":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/2828346?format=cv","citations":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/?q=refersto%3Arecid%3A2828346"},"created":"2024-09-16T04:04:53.379986+00:00","updated":"2026-07-24T14:07:16.775480+00:00","id":"2828346","metadata":{"authors":[{"raw_affiliations":[{"value":"School of Physics and Astronomy, University of Leeds, Leeds, UK"}],"affiliations_identifiers":[{"schema":"GRID","value":"grid.9909.9"},{"schema":"ROR","value":"https://ror.org/024mrxd33"}],"full_name_unicode_normalized":"jeyaretnam, jared","full_name":"Jeyaretnam, Jared","record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/authors/2750787"},"ids":[{"schema":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-8316-9025"},{"schema":"INSPIRE BAI","value":"J.A.Jeyaretnam.1"}],"last_name":"Jeyaretnam","affiliations":[{"record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/institutions/903524"},"value":"Leeds U."}],"uuid":"0c48ac40-16a5-47d9-95c3-c5c9588d81f4","first_name":"Jared","recid":2750787},{"raw_affiliations":[{"value":"School of Physics and Astronomy, University of Leeds, Leeds, UK"}],"affiliations_identifiers":[{"schema":"GRID","value":"grid.9909.9"},{"schema":"ROR","value":"https://ror.org/024mrxd33"}],"full_name_unicode_normalized":"bhore, tanmay","full_name":"Bhore, Tanmay","record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/authors/2885857"},"ids":[{"schema":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-9304-7144"},{"schema":"INSPIRE BAI","value":"T.Bhore.2"}],"last_name":"Bhore","affiliations":[{"record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/institutions/903524"},"value":"Leeds U."}],"uuid":"fca7044b-ed0a-4098-b817-2276938cb780","first_name":"Tanmay","recid":2885857},{"raw_affiliations":[{"value":"School of Mathematics and Physics, The University of Queensland, St. Lucia, QLD, Australia"}],"affiliations_identifiers":[{"schema":"GRID","value":"grid.1003.2"},{"schema":"ROR","value":"https://ror.org/00rqy9422"}],"full_name_unicode_normalized":"osborne, jesse j.","full_name":"Osborne, Jesse J.","curated_relation":true,"record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/authors/3182111"}